{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788126745,"nonce":"Qq58ZuFEmuXz6qA+Bm2T5Z3NO0w=","message":"SESSION 3 (dorian's Linux box) — review of session 2, two remediation issues\nfiled, and one spec ambiguity that needs your call.\n\nThree reviewers (two opus on the code, one sonnet on process). Verdict on the\nfive closed specs: **good work, above average** — hook points chosen from\ndecompiled source rather than guessed, observer discipline holds under\ninspection, the tick loop matches FINDINGS section 9 exactly, and the closing\ncomments are unusually honest. Process was clean: no hidden scope, no\nsource+DLL mixing, five standalone `Build:` commits, nothing left behind.\n\nBut both code reviewers landed on \"would not merge as-is\", and the defects are\nreal. Filed as **1.5 (4b65a28)** and **2.6 (3dce29a)**, both `state:next`,\n`agent:opus`. Neither requires reverting shipped work.\n\nThe important structural point: **2.6 gates 2.2 and 2.4**, which are told to\ncopy 2.1's exemplar — and the exemplar has an uncommented per-colonist lazy\nroom analysis, an uncapped list, and a `DangerWatcher` hazard that a copy of\nthe pattern would trip. Both were moved to `state:backlog` with GATED comments.\n2.6 also gates 3.2/3.3, which build UX on `find-rect` — and `find-rect`\ncurrently returns candidates that are not the nearest, visible in 2.3's own\nclosing evidence (`near=[114,133]` returned dist 2.2 when dist 0.0 existed).\n\nThe Windows-built DLL loads and runs correctly here: full protocol round-trip,\n`digest` 1201 bytes, `advance ticks:60000` exact and returning paused, 0 red\nerrors / same 9-warning baseline. **No Linux/Windows behavioral divergence in\nthe substrate.** A 3000-tick advance reporting 1123 tps against a 1000 cap\nturned out to be a short-window artifact — 60000 ticks held 1000.84.\n\nTwo genuine box-to-box differences: the source builds clean on Linux but the\ntwo boxes' binaries share only ~52% of their bytes (different Roslyn), so\nbinary comparison is not a parity check — behavioural testing is the only one.\nAnd `Journal.cs` uses `WriteLine`, so journals are LF here and CRLF on BORGES;\nthat matters for 5.1, which makes the journal its assertion substrate.\n\n**Fog of war is not mentioned in spec 2.3 and got resolved three different ways\nin one file.** `nearest` skips fogged things; `map-view` renders full terrain\nand thing detail under undiscovered fog; `find-rect` will hand back a\n\"buildable\" candidate in unexplored ground. On a fresh crashlanded map that is\nmost of the map. Three defensible answers — the agent sees what the player sees\n(honest, harder); the agent sees everything (simpler, cheats); per-verb flag —\nand it changes 3.2/3.3's contract, so it is not the worker's to pick. Parked in\n2.6 as a question rather than resolved.\n\n`_RimWorld-Test` re-rooting bug; catalog.py cross-check (only doable on this\nbox); WirelessChargingMech missing on BORGES; Storefront/Guests version clash.","files":null}]}